# Mod-event integration stages and draft attachment cleanup

Assignment: release swarm `mod-events`, tracked under FD-266. Baseline:
`673cc43fad4122413ed5c1582c67656649286807`.

## Ruby reference and behavior

The installed VM sources were read from card 1.110.0 and
card-mod-carrierwave 0.20.0. `lib/card/director/phases.rb:48-51` runs
`integrate`, `after_integrate`, then `integrate_with_delay` after commit.
`lib/card/director/run.rb` walks the subdirectors within each stage.

Carrierwave's `set/abstract/attachment/00_upload_cache.rb:62-64` declares
`clear_draft_files` on create, at `integrate_with_delay`, priority 100.
Its body calls `delete_tmp_files_of_cached_uploads` in `set/all/file_utils.rb`:
select File/Image drafts, retain uploads no more than five days old and
read-only cards, remove each selected attachment's files, then delete its
draft action. Acts and change rows remain.

## Implementation

The action engine dispatches both missing stages for create, update, and
delete after the existing integrate work. It finishes the parent's and
subcards' `after_integrate` callbacks before entering their delayed stage.
Finalized card snapshots retain dirty fields and receiver membership for
the later stages. Aborted draft saves remain outside integration.

Delayed mod bodies use the existing delayed-job queue when delaying is
enabled; the job performer executes the selected body. Existing follower
hooks retain their own dispatch ownership, avoiding duplicate jobs.
Engines without late-stage registrations perform no extra late-stage reads.

`src/card/attachments/clear-draft-files.ts` implements the carrierwave body
against the real database and attachment-store removal capability. Cache-card
drafts use the tmp area; existing-card drafts use the files area. Removal
includes image renditions. A storage error retains the draft action and is
visible in the mod-event failure report.

Cleanup qualifies local identifiers against their draft action/card IDs and
ages the draft using its act timestamp. Ruby's source query joins `SELECT *`
over cards and actions; explicit identities and the draft's own timestamp
protect ownership and recent uploads here. ActiveRecord's handling of
overlapping joined columns was not reproduced. Web-backed cards and
unrecognized identifiers remain retained; cloud/coded deletion is outside
this local-identifier cleanup implementation.

